# Example 15: Configure the App via an Env File. (co-12)
#
# Non-secret configuration lives in the ENVIRONMENT, not in the repo. This file
# holds the app's tunables (log level, port, feature flag); the systemd unit
# (Example 62) or the app launcher reads it. Permissions are locked to the
# deploy user so only the service can read it.
#
# Install as /opt/myapp/app.env (mode 0600, owned by deploy). This file is the
# canonical ".env.example" shape: values shown are SAFE NON-SECRETS. Real
# secrets go in Example 16's separate, never-committed file.

# --- App tunables (non-secret; safe to read into a commit) --------------------
APP_HOST=127.0.0.1  # => co-12: bind loopback; the proxy (Example 12) is the public face
APP_PORT=8000  # => co-12: the local port the proxy forwards to
APP_LOG_LEVEL=info  # => co-12/co-14: controls verbosity in the journal
APP_FEATURE_NEW_CACHE=false  # => co-12: a feature flag, toggled per-environment

# --- The one rule this file ENFORCES: no secrets here -------------------------
# A DATABASE_URL with a password, an API key, a signing secret -- NONE of those
# belong here. This file is read by the app AND lives next to the repo; a secret
# here is a secret one git-add away from public. Put secrets in Example 16's
# separate, mode-0600, gitignored file instead.

# --- Install + lock permissions (run on the box) ------------------------------
#   install -o deploy -g deploy -m 600 app.env /opt/myapp/app.env
#   chown deploy:deploy /opt/myapp/app.env
# --- Verify the app reads it (Example 62 wires it into the systemd unit) ------
#   sudo -u deploy bash -c 'set -a; . /opt/myapp/app.env; env | grep ^APP_'
# [verify] the four APP_* variables are present in the app's environment.